  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 225 AM CST Tue Nov 3 2026\n\nEarly morning surface analysis depicts a pair of low pressure \nsystems across the Plains and upper Midwest, one across \nnortheast MN and the other across south-central KS. The southern\nlow is forecast to lift northeast into eastern IA through this \nevening, before deepening to around 997 mb and continuing into \nwestern WI by early Wednesday. This deepening is in response to \na strong mid-level short wave trough rotating around the base of\nthe larger scale upper trough across the central CONUS. \nEventually, the mid-level closed low is progged to become \nvertically stacked above the occluding surface cyclone and drift\neast-northeast across the northern Great Lakes region Wednesday\nnight into Thursday.\n\nIn the near term, a broad region of enhanced low-level \nsoutherly flow was in place east of the low across the Midwest. \nWarm/moist advection on south winds gusting to 20-25 mph \ncontinue to produce an extensive low-stratus deck and drizzle at\nthis hour, in addition to unseasonably warm overnight temps in \nthe low-60s. Current obs trends indicate cloud bases lifting \nfrom south to north, which will end the drizzle early this \nmorning. The tight surface pressure gradient will continue to \nsupport strong/gusty southerly winds across the area today into \ntonight, with gusts 35-40 mph especially later this morning \nthrough this afternoon/early evening. Depending on how deeply \nthe boundary layer can mix, 40+ mph gusts are possible \nespecially across our southern/eastern counties where cloud \ncover looks to be thinner allowing warmer low-mid 70s temps. \nFarther north/northwest, more cloud cover and increasing shower \nchances may limit temps to the mid-upper 60s. \n\nLarge scale forcing will begin to increase later this \nmorning/today however, as the strong Plains short wave lifts \nnortheast into the region. Rain/showers and some embedded \nthunderstorms (currently evident across MO/southern IA) will \nspread into the forecast area from the southwest later this \nmorning into this afternoon. This strongest forcing continues to\nappear to be focused over the area this evening and overnight, \nahead of the slightly negatively-tilted wave and approaching \nsurface cold front. The combination of this forcing, deep \nmoisture (PWATs approaching 1.70\" or nearly 300% of normal) and \nnorth-south mid-level frontogenetic banding, will likely support\nfairly widespread rain amounts in excess of a half inch, with \nsome locally higher amounts especially across the western half \nof the forecast area.\n\nRainfall will taper off from west to east Wednesday morning \nbehind the cold front, though scattered showers may linger at \ntimes into the end of the week as the upper trough drifts slowly\nover the area. In fact, the upper trough is progged to be slow \nto exit to the east through the end of the week, with some \nguidance (notably the EC/ECE) keeping the remnant upper level \nlow across the Great Lakes region through Friday. Eventually \nmid-level short wave ridging should develop behind the departing\ntrough, at least temporarily, with current ensembles indicating\ndry weather from late Friday into Saturday. Additional \nshortwaves rippling into the region in northwest flow aloft \nlater in the weekend may bring back some scattered shower \nchances in the Sunday-Monday timeframe. \n\nTemperature-wise, after well-above average warmth today, \nreadings will trend cooler behind the cold front on Wednesday \nwith highs in the upper 50s/low 60s. Daytime highs in the 50s \nand lows in the 30s/40s are expected Thursday through the \nweekend, which are around our averages for this time of year.\n\nRatzer",
